    4466: "Ῥαγαῦ (Rhagaû | hrag-ow')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7466)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Ragau (i.e. Reu), a patriarch<BR><B>KJV:</B> Ragau.<BR><I>See:</I> H7466<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4463 (beat), G4464 (rod), G4465 (serjeant)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥαγαῦ, Ῥαγαύ<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7466 רְעוּ",
    4467: "ῥᾳδιούργημα (rhaidioúrgēma | hrad-ee-oorg'-ay-mah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a comparative of ῥᾴδιος (easy, i.e. reckless) and G2041<BR><B>Strong's:</B> easy-going behavior, i.e. (by extension) a crime<BR><B>KJV:</B> lewdness.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G2041<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4468 (mischief), G4467 (lewdness)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥᾳδιούργημα, ῥάδιούργημα",
    4468: "ῥᾳδιουργία (rhaidiourgía | hrad-ee-oorg-ee'-a)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from the same as G4467<BR><B>Strong's:</B> recklessness, i.e. (by extension) malignity<BR><B>KJV:</B> mischief.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4467<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4468 (mischief), G4467 (lewdness)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥᾳδιουργία, ῥάδιουργία",
    4469: "ῥακά (rhaká | rhak-ah')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Chaldee origin (compare H7386)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> O empty one, i.e. thou worthless (as a term of utter vilification)<BR><B>KJV:</B> Raca.<BR><I>See:</I> H7386<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4469 (Raca)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥακά, ῥαχά<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7386 רֵיק",
    4470: "ῥάκος (rhákos | hrak'-os)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4486<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a \"rag,\" i.e. piece of cloth<BR><B>KJV:</B> cloth.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4486<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4469 (Raca)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥάκος<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H899 בֶּגֶד, H5499 סְחָבָה",
    4471: "Ῥαμᾶ (Rhamâ | hram-ah')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7414)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Rama (i.e. Ramah), a place in Palestine<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rama.<BR><I>See:</I> H7414<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4469 (Raca)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥαμᾶ, Ῥαμά<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7413 רָמָה, H7414 רָמָה",
    4472: "ῥαντίζω (rhantízō | hran-tid'-zo)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a derivative of ῥαίνω (to sprinkle)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to render besprinkled, i.e. asperse (ceremonially or figuratively)<BR><B>KJV:</B>  sprinkle.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥαντίζω<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H2398 חָטָא, H5137 נָזָה",
    4473: "ῥαντισμός (rhantismós | hran-tis-mos')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4472<BR><B>Strong's:</B> aspersion (ceremonially or figuratively)<BR><B>KJV:</B> sprinkling.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4472<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥαντισμός<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H5079 נִדָּה",
    4474: "ῥαπίζω (rhapízō | hrap-id'-zo)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a derivative of a primary ῥέπω (to let fall, \"rap\")<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to slap<BR><B>KJV:</B> smite (with the palm of the hand). Compare G5180 .<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G5180<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥαπίζω",
    4475: "ῥάπισμα (rhápisma | hrap'-is-mah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4474<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a slap<BR><B>KJV:</B> (+ strike with the) palm of the hand, smite with the hand.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4474<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥάπισμα<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H4803 מָרַט",
    4476: "ῥαφίς (rhaphís | hraf-ece')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a primary ῥάπτω (to sew<BR><B>Strong's:</B> perhaps rather akin to the base of G4474 through the idea of puncturing); a needle<BR><B>KJV:</B>  needle.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4474<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4473 (sprinkling), G4472 (sprinkle)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥαφίς, βελόνη",
    4477: "Ῥαχάβ (Rhacháb | hrakh-ab')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from the same as G4460<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Rachab, a Canaanitess<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rachab.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4460<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥαχάβ",
    4478: "Ῥαχήλ (Rhachḗl | hrakh-ale')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7354)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Rachel, the wife of Jacob<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rachel.<BR><I>See:</I> H7354<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥαχήλ<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7354 רָחֵל",
    4479: "Ῥεβέκκα (Rhebékka | hreb-bek'-kah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7259)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Rebecca (i.e. Ribkah), the wife of Isaac<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rebecca.<BR><I>See:</I> H7259<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥεβέκκα<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7259 רִבְקָה",
    4480: "ῥέδα (rhéda | hred'-ah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Latin origin<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a rheda, i.e. four-wheeled carriage (wagon for riding)<BR><B>KJV:</B> chariot.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥέδα, ῥέδη",
    4481: "Ῥεμφάν (Rhemphán | hrem-fan')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> by incorrect transliteration for a word of Hebrew origin (H3594)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Remphan (i.e. Kijun), an Egyptian idol<BR><B>KJV:</B> Remphan.<BR><I>See:</I> H3594<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥεμφάν, Ῥαιφάν, Ῥεφάν, Ῥομφά<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H3594 כִּיּוּן",
    4482: "ῥέω (rhéō | hreh'-o)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> a primary verb<BR><B>Strong's:</B> for some tenses of which a prolonged form ῥεύω (rheúō | hryoo'-o)<BR> is used to flow (\"run\"; as water)<BR><B>KJV:</B> flow.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥέω<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H1980 הָלַךְ, H2100 זוּב, H4743 מָקַק, H5107 נוּב, H5140 נָזַל, H7491 רָעַף",
    4483: "ῥέω (rhéō | hreh'-o), for certain tenses of which a prolonged form ἐρέω (eréō | er-eh'-o)<BR><BR> is used; and both as alternate for G2036<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> perhaps akin (or identical) with G4482 (through the idea of pouring forth)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to utter, i.e. speak or say<BR><B>KJV:</B> command, make, say, speak (of). Compare G3004 .<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G2036<BR><I>See:</I> G4482<BR><I>See:</I> G3004<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥέω, λέγω",
    4484: "Ῥήγιον (Rhḗgion | hrayg'-ee-on)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Latin origin<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Rhegium, a place in Italy<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rhegium.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥήγιον",
    4485: "ῥῆγμα (rhēgma | hrayg'-mah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4486<BR><B>Strong's:</B> something torn, i.e. a fragment (by implication and abstractly, a fall)<BR><B>KJV:</B> ruin.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4486<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥῆγμα<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H1233 בְּקִיעַ, H7168 קֶרַע",
    4486: "ῥήγνυμι (rhḗgnymi | hrayg'-noo-mee) or ῥήσσω (rhḗssō | hrace'-so)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> both prolonged forms of ῥήκω (which appears only in certain forms, and is itself probably a strengthened form of ἄγνυμι (see in G2608)) to \"break,\" \"wreck\" or \"crack\", i.e. (especially) to sunder (by separation of the parts<BR><B>Strong's:</B> G2608 being its intensive (with the preposition in composition), and G2352 a shattering to minute fragments; but not a reduction to the constituent particles, like G3089) or disrupt, lacerate; by implication, to convulse (with spasms); figuratively, to give vent to joyful emotions<BR><B>KJV:</B> break (forth), burst, rend, tear.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G2608<BR><I>See:</I> G2608<BR><I>See:</I> G2352<BR><I>See:</I> G3089<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4477 (Rachab)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥήγνυμι, ῥάσσω, ῥήσσω<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H1234 בָּקַע, H2040 הָרַס, H4998 נָאָה, H5423 נָתַק, H6476 פָּצַח, H7121 קָרָא",
    4487: "ῥῆμα (rhēma | hray'-mah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4483<BR><B>Strong's:</B> an utterance (individually, collectively or specially),; by implication, a matter or topic (especially of narration, command or dispute); with a negative naught whatever<BR><B>KJV:</B> + evil, + nothing, saying, word.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4483<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4487 (+ evil)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥῆμα<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H561 אֵמֶר, H1681 דִּבָּה, H1697 דָּבָר, H4405 מִלָּה, H4687 מִצְוָה, H4853 מַשָּׂא, H6310 פֶּה, H6600 פִּתְגָּם",
    4488: "Ῥησά (Rhēsá | hray-sah')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> probably of Hebrew origin (apparently for H7509)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Resa (i.e. Rephajah), an Israelite<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rhesa.<BR><I>See:</I> H7509<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4487 (+ evil)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥησά<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7509 רְפָיָה",
    4489: "ῥήτωρ (rhḗtōr | hray'-tore)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4483<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a speaker, i.e. (by implication) a forensic advocate<BR><B>KJV:</B> orator.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4483<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4487 (+ evil)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥήτωρ",
    4490: "ῥητῶς (rhētōs | hray-toce')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> adverb from a derivative of G4483<BR><B>Strong's:</B> out-spokenly, i.e. distinctly<BR><B>KJV:</B> expressly.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4483<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4487 (+ evil)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥητῶς",
    4491: "ῥίζα (rhíza | hrid'-zah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> apparently a primary word<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a \"root\" (literally or figuratively)<BR><B>KJV:</B> root.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4492 (root), G4491 (root)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥίζα<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H3 אֵב, H1503 גֶּזַע, H3653 כֵּן, H4351 מְכוּרָה, H8327 שָׁרַשׁ, H8327 שָׁרַשׁ, H8330 שֹׁרֶשׁ",
    4492: "ῥιζόω (rhizóō | hrid-zo'-o)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4491<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to root (figuratively, become stable)<BR><B>KJV:</B> root.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4491<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4492 (root), G4491 (root)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥιζόω, ῥιζόομαι<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H8327 שָׁרַשׁ",
    4493: "ῥιπή (rhipḗ | hree-pay')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4496<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a jerk (of the eye, i.e. (by analogy) an instant)<BR><B>KJV:</B> twinkling.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4496<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4492 (root), G4491 (root)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥιπή, ῥοπή<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H6425 פֶּלֶס, H7834 שַׁחַק",
    4494: "ῥιπίζω (rhipízō | hrip-id'-zo)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a derivative of G4496 (meaning a fan or bellows)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to breeze up, i.e. (by analogy) to agitate (into waves)<BR><B>KJV:</B> toss.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4496<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4492 (root), G4491 (root)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥιπίζω, ῥιπίζομαι<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H5376 נְשָׂא",
    4495: "ῥιπτέω (rhiptéō | hrip-teh'-o)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a derivative of G4496<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to toss up<BR><B>KJV:</B> cast off.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4496<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥιπτέω",
    4496: "ῥίπτω (rhíptō | hrip'-to)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> a primary verb (perhaps rather akin to the base of G4474, through the idea of sudden motion)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to fling (properly, with a quick toss, thus differing from G906, which denotes a deliberate hurl; and from τείνω (see in G1614), which indicates an extended projection); by qualification, to deposit (as if a load); by extension, to disperse<BR><B>KJV:</B> cast (down, out), scatter abroad, throw.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4474<BR><I>See:</I> G906<BR><I>See:</I> G1614<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥίπτω, ῥίπτω/ῥιπτέω<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7993 שָׁלַךְ",
    4497: "Ῥοβοάμ (Rhoboám | hrob-o-am')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7346)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Roboam (i.e. Rechobam), an Israelite<BR><B>KJV:</B> Roboam.<BR><I>See:</I> H7346<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥοβοάμ<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7346 רְחַבְעָם",
    4498: "Ῥόδη (Rhódē | hrod'-ay)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> probably for rhode (a rose)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Rode, a servant girl<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rhoda.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥόδη",
    4499: "Ῥόδος (Rhódos | hrod'-os)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> probably from ῥόδον (a rose)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Rhodus, an island of the Mediterranean<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rhodes.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥόδος",
    4500: "ῥοιζηδόν (rhoizēdón | hroyd-zay-don')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> adverb from a derivative of ῥοῖζος (a whir)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> whizzingly, i.e. with a crash<BR><B>KJV:</B> with a great noise.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4495 (cast off), G641 (cast), G1977 (cast upon), G4496 (cast)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥοιζηδόν",
    4501: "ῥομφαία (rhomphaía | hrom-fah'-yah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> probably of foreign origin<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a sabre, i.e. a long and broad cutlass (any weapon of the kind, literally or figuratively)<BR><B>KJV:</B> sword.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4501 (sword)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥομφαία<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H2595 חֲנִית, H2719 חֶרֶב, H3979 מַאֲכֶלֶת",
    4502: "Ῥουβήν (Rhoubḗn | hroo-bane')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7205)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Ruben (i.e. Reuben), an Israelite<BR><B>KJV:</B> Reuben.<BR><I>See:</I> H7205<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4501 (sword)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥουβήν, Ῥουβίμ<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7205 רְאוּבֵן",
    4503: "Ῥούθ (Rhoúth | hrooth)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7327)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Ruth, a Moabitess<BR><B>KJV:</B> Ruth.<BR><I>See:</I> H7327<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4503 (Ruth)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥούθ<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7327 רוּת",
    4504: "Ῥοῦφος (Rhoûphos | hroo'-fos)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Latin origin<BR><B>Strong's:</B> red; Rufus, a Christian<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rufus.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4503 (Ruth)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥοῦφος",
    4505: "ῥύμη (rhýmē | hroo'-may)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> prolongation from G4506 in its original sense<BR><B>Strong's:</B> an alley or avenue (as crowded)<BR><B>KJV:</B> lane, street.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4506<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4503 (Ruth)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥύμη<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7339 רְחֹב",
    4506: "ῥύομαι (rhýomai | rhoo'-om-ahee)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> middle voice of an obsolete verb, akin to G4482 (through the idea of a current<BR><B>Strong's:</B> compare G4511); to rush or draw (for oneself), i.e. rescue<BR><B>KJV:</B> deliver(-er).<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4482<BR><I>See:</I> G4511<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥύομαι<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H1350 גָּאַל, H2502 חָלַץ, H3467 יָשַׁע, H4422 מָלַט, H5337 נָצַל, H5341 נָצַר, H6299 פָּדָה, H6403 פָּלַט, H6475 פָּצָה, H7804 שְׁזַב",
    4507: "ῥυπαρία (rhyparía | hroo-par-ee'-ah)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4508<BR><B>Strong's:</B> dirtiness (morally)<BR><B>KJV:</B> turpitude.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4508<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥυπαρία",
    4508: "ῥυπαρός (rhyparós | rhoo-par-os')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4509<BR><B>Strong's:</B> dirty, i.e. (relatively) cheap or shabby; morally, wicked<BR><B>KJV:</B> vile.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4509<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥυπαρός<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H6674 צֹוא",
    4509: "ῥύπος (rhýpos | hroo'-pos)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of uncertain affinity<BR><B>Strong's:</B> dirt, i.e. (morally) depravity<BR><B>KJV:</B> filth.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥύπος<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H2931 טָמֵא, H6675 צֹואָה, H7845 שַׁחַת",
    4510: "ῥυπόω (rhypóō | rhoo-po'-o)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4509<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to soil, i.e. (intransitively) to become dirty (morally)<BR><B>KJV:</B> be filthy.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4509<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥυπόω, ῥυπαίνω, ῥυπαρεύω, ῥυπαρεύομαι",
    4511: "ῥύσις (rhýsis | hroo'-sis)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4506 in the sense of its congener G4482<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a flux (of blood)<BR><B>KJV:</B> issue.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4506<BR><I>See:</I> G4482<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥύσις<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H2100 זוּב, H2101 זֹוב, H4726 מָקֹור, H7137 קָרֶה, H8585 תְּעָלָה",
    4512: "ῥυτίς (rhytís | hroo-tece')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4506<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a fold (as drawing together), i.e. a wrinkle (especially on the face)<BR><B>KJV:</B> wrinkle.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4506<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥυτίς",
    4513: "Ῥωμαϊκός (Rhōmaïkós | rho-mah-ee-kos')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4514<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Romaic, i.e. Latin<BR><B>KJV:</B> Latin.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4514<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥωμαϊκός G4869, G4870",
    4514: "Ῥωμαῖος (Rhōmaîos | hro-mah'-yos)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from G4516<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Romæan, i.e. Roman (as noun)<BR><B>KJV:</B> Roman, of Rome.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4516<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥωμαῖος",
    4515: "Ῥωμαϊστί (Rhōmaïstí | hro-mah-is-tee')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> adverb from a presumed derivative of G4516<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Romaistically, i.e. in the Latin language<BR><B>KJV:</B> Latin.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4516<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥωμαϊστί",
    4516: "Ῥώμη (Rhṓmē | hro'-may)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from the base of G4517<BR><B>Strong's:</B> strength; Roma, the capital of Italy<BR><B>KJV:</B> Rome.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4517<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Ῥώμη",
    4517: "ῥώννυμι (rhṓnnymi | hrone'-noo-mee)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> prolongation from ῥώομαι (to dart<BR><B>Strong's:</B> probably akin to G4506); to strengthen, i.e. (impersonal passive) have health (as a parting exclamation, good-bye)<BR><B>KJV:</B> farewell.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4506<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> ῥώννυμι, ῥώννυμαι",
    4518: "σαβαχθάνι (sabachtháni | sab-akh-than-ee')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Chaldee or (H7662 with pronominal suffix)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> thou hast left me; sabachthani (i.e. shebakthani), a cry of distress<BR><B>KJV:</B> sabachthani.<BR><I>See:</I> H7662<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> σαβαχθάνι, ζαφθάνι, σαβαχθανι, σαβαχθανί<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7662 שְׁבַק",
    4519: "σαβαώθ (sabaṓth | sab-ah-owth')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H6635 in feminine plural)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> armies; sabaoth (i.e. tsebaoth), a military epithet of God<BR><B>KJV:</B> sabaoth.<BR><I>See:</I> H6635<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4506 (deliver)<BR><B>Variants:</B> σαβαώθ, Σαβαώθ<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H6635 צָבָא",
    4520: "σαββατισμός (sabbatismós | sab-bat-is-mos')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a derivative of G4521<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a \"sabbatism\", i.e. (figuratively) the repose of Christianity (as a type of heaven)<BR><B>KJV:</B> rest.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4521<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4521 (sabbath), G4520 (rest)<BR><B>Variants:</B> σαββατισμός",
    4521: "σάββατον (sábbaton | sab'-bat-on)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H7676)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> the Sabbath (i.e. Shabbath), or day of weekly repose from secular avocations (also the observance or institution itself); by extension, a se'nnight, i.e. the interval between two Sabbaths; likewise the plural in all the above applications<BR><B>KJV:</B> sabbath (day), week.<BR><I>See:</I> H7676<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4521 (sabbath), G4520 (rest)<BR><B>Variants:</B> σάββατον<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H7676 שַׁבָּת, H7677 שַׁבָּתֹון",
    4522: "σαγήνη (sagḗnē | sag-ay'-nay)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> from a derivative of σάττω (to equip) meaning furniture, especially a pack-saddle (which in the East is merely a bag of netted rope)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a \"seine\" for fishing<BR><B>KJV:</B> net.<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4521 (sabbath), G4520 (rest)<BR><B>Variants:</B> σαγήνη<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H2764 חֵרֶם, H4365 מִכְמֶרֶת",
    4523: "Σαδδουκαῖος (Saddoukaîos | sad-doo-kah'-yos)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> probably from G4524<BR><B>Strong's:</B> a Sadducæan (i.e. Tsadokian), or follower of a certain heretical Israelite<BR><B>KJV:</B> Sadducee.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4524<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4523 (Sadducee)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Σαδδουκαῖος",
    4524: "Σαδώκ (Sadṓk | sad-oke')<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H6659)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> Sadoc (i.e. Tsadok), an Israelite<BR><B>KJV:</B> Sadoc.<BR><I>See:</I> H6659<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4523 (Sadducee)<BR><B>Variants:</B> Σαδώκ<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H6659 צָדֹוק",
    4525: "σαίνω (saínō | sah'-ee-no)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> akin to G4579<BR><B>Strong's:</B> to wag (as a dog its tail fawningly), i.e. (generally) to shake (figuratively, disturb)<BR><B>KJV:</B> move.<BR><BR><I>See:</I> G4579<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4525 (move)<BR><B>Variants:</B> σαίνω, σιαίνομαι, σαίνομαι",
    4526: "σάκκος (sákkos | sak'-kos)<BR><BR><B>Derivation:</B> of Hebrew origin (H8242)<BR><B>Strong's:</B> \"sack\"-cloth, i.e. mohair (the material or garments made of it, worn as a sign of grief)<BR><B>KJV:</B>  sackcloth.<BR><I>See:</I> H8242<BR><BR><B>Cognate Group:</B> G4526 (sackcloth)<BR><B>Variants:</B> σάκκος<BR><B>Hebrew Equivalents:</B> H8242 שַׂק",
